{.a} should be interpreted as "OR/AND" rather than the exclusive "OR" (XOR).
(1) says that:

Alice drinks tea OR/AND coffee | AND | John drinks tea OR/AND coffee.

Alice may drink either "tea OR coffee" or "tea AND coffee". The same goes
for John. So, we can't assume "Alice drinks (exclusively) tea and John
drinks (exclusively) coffee", and vice versa. {.a} in (1) allows both (2)
and (3) to be true.

